## Mocking in Unit Testing

## Why Mock?

Unit tests should test ONE thing in isolation. Real dependencies make tests:
- Slow (network/database round trips)
- Flaky (dependent on external state)
- Complex to set up (database seeding, API keys)
- Non-deterministic (different results each run)

Mocking replaces dependencies with controlled, predictable doubles.

## Types of Test Doubles

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **Dummy** | Passed but never used (fills parameter list) |
| **Stub** | Returns hardcoded values |
| **Mock** | Records interactions + can assert on them |
| **Spy** | Wraps real implementation, records calls |
| **Fake** | Working simplified implementation (in-memory DB) |

## Jest Mocking Examples

```typescript
// jest.fn() — mock function
const sendEmail = jest.fn();
sendEmail('user@example.com');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith('user@example.com');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);

// Return value
const fetchUser = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' });

// jest.mock() — mock an entire module
jest.mock('@/lib/email', () => ({
  sendEmail: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ success: true }),
}));

// jest.spyOn() — spy on a real method
const spy = jest.spyOn(console, 'error').mockImplementation(() => {});
// ... test that triggers console.error ...
expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith('Expected error');
spy.mockRestore();
```
